From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Burke Cates <burke.cates@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 18:56:22 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 24/27] NOT-FOR-UPSTREAM: soundwire: qcom: re-initialise the multi-master
 dependent behind the primary

At probe the primary initialises the dependent controller after its own
frame is running. After that the dependent is never touched again: it has
no bus of its own and no runtime PM, and the primary's resume paths only
re-program MM_SYNC on it. Its registers survive system sleep, but its
data ports do not stay lined up with the primary's frame.

On the ASUS Zenbook A16 the WCD9378 headphone port is fed from swr1 (the
dependent of swr2). With bank switches sent through both controllers,
the left/right orientation is correct on every stream start after boot,
but after an s2idle cycle it comes back swapped about half the time and
then stays swapped for every later start until the next suspend: 14 of
29 cycles (held stream or not), checked on a line-in loopback. The
dependent's COMP_CFG, MM_SYNC_CONFIG, frame control and MCP_STATUS read
the same before and after.

Initialise the dependent again whenever the primary's frame restarts:
after a clock-stop exit, after the reset-based resume and after a bus
recovery, the way probe does it. With that, 16 of 16 s2idle cycles came
out correct (6 with the headphone stream held open across suspend, 4
with a speaker stream held, 2 with the headset mic capturing, 4 idle;
38 of 38 starts after them), as did a debug build's 12 of 12.

Assisted-by: Claude Code (claude-opus-5-5)
---
Not-for-upstream: local workaround for one laptop, not reviewed for
  submission; use only for local testing.

 drivers/soundwire/qcom.c | 30 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
index c6823c77fb0c..f18e0621e3c5 100644
--- a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
@@ -826,6 +826,7 @@ static irqreturn_t qcom_swrm_wake_irq_handler(int irq, void *dev_id)
 }
 
 static int qcom_swrm_init(struct qcom_swrm_ctrl *ctrl);
+static int qcom_swrm_init_peer(struct qcom_swrm_ctrl *ctrl);
 static void qcom_swrm_reprobe_unattached_slaves(struct qcom_swrm_ctrl *ctrl);
 
 static bool qcom_swrm_all_slaves_online(struct qcom_swrm_ctrl *ctrl)
@@ -924,6 +925,9 @@ static void qcom_swrm_recover(struct qcom_swrm_ctrl *ctrl, bool reprobe)
 		goto recovery_failed;
 	usleep_range(2000, 2050);
 	ret = qcom_swrm_init(ctrl);
+	if (ret)
+		goto recovery_failed;
+	ret = qcom_swrm_init_peer(ctrl);
 	if (ret)
 		goto recovery_failed;
 	qcom_swrm_sync_bank(ctrl);
@@ -1458,6 +1462,21 @@ static int qcom_swrm_init(struct qcom_swrm_ctrl *ctrl)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Bring a multi-master dependent up again behind its primary, as probe
+ * does. Across system sleep the dependent keeps its registers, but its
+ * data ports can come back at a fixed offset from the primary's frame
+ * that no bank switch corrects; initialising it again once the primary's
+ * frame runs lines it up. Do it whenever the primary's frame restarts.
+ */
+static int qcom_swrm_init_peer(struct qcom_swrm_ctrl *ctrl)
+{
+	if (!ctrl->is_primary || !ctrl->peer_ctrl)
+		return 0;
+
+	return qcom_swrm_init(ctrl->peer_ctrl);
+}
+
 static int qcom_swrm_read_prop(struct sdw_bus *bus)
 {
 	struct qcom_swrm_ctrl *ctrl = to_qcom_sdw(bus);
@@ -2512,8 +2531,7 @@ static int qcom_swrm_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	if (ret)
 		goto err_master_add;
 
-	if (ctrl->is_primary && ctrl->peer_ctrl)
-		qcom_swrm_init(ctrl->peer_ctrl);
+	qcom_swrm_init_peer(ctrl);
 
 	if (!wait_event_timeout(ctrl->slaves_wait,
 				qcom_swrm_all_slaves_online(ctrl),
@@ -2653,6 +2671,10 @@ static int __maybe_unused swrm_runtime_resume(struct device *dev)
 		if (ret)
 			goto err_clk;
 
+		ret = qcom_swrm_init_peer(ctrl);
+		if (ret)
+			goto err_clk;
+
 		/* wait for hw enumeration to complete */
 		if (!wait_for_completion_timeout(&ctrl->enumeration,
 						 msecs_to_jiffies(TIMEOUT_MS))) {
@@ -2718,6 +2740,10 @@ static int __maybe_unused swrm_runtime_resume(struct device *dev)
 		if (ret)
 			goto err_clk;
 
+		ret = qcom_swrm_init_peer(ctrl);
+		if (ret)
+			goto err_clk;
+
 		ret = sdw_bus_exit_clk_stop(&ctrl->bus);
 		if (ret < 0)
 			goto err_clk;
